a font can be asked for one attribute, and a descriptor is never nothing

CTFontCopyAttribute was a stub returning nil. A stub return is a policy answer rather than an
absence, and this one was the whole reason Money Manager EX could not start. From its own
disassembly, wxNativeFontInfo::GetCTWeight is

  movq  _kCTFontTraitsAttribute, %rsi
  callq _CTFontCopyAttribute
  movq  _kCTFontWeightTrait, %rsi
  callq _CFDictionaryGetValue        on whatever came back, with no check in between

so nil became a null dictionary and the process died inside CoreFoundation, eleven frames from
anything that names a font.

It is implemented as macOS documents it: one attribute of a font is the same attribute of its
descriptor. That works here without keeping anything in step, because a descriptor in this port IS
the attribute dictionary.

AND CTFontCopyFontDescriptor NOW ALWAYS ANSWERS for a non-null font. It used to return NULL when the
font had no family name, which macOS never does and which no caller expects. An unnamed font gets a
descriptor with no family entry, which is the truthful shape.

THE THREE NUMERIC TRAITS ARE NOT OPTIONAL EITHER. macOS puts weight, width and slant in every font
derived traits dictionary. A caller that reads one and hands the result to CFNumberGetValue has
nothing to check against, so leaving them out moves the crash rather than avoiding it. They are
derived from the symbolic traits, which is all this port knows: normalised, 0 meaning regular.

